        "content": "The image contains a single problem. Let me solve it.\n\n**Problem:** Compute $\\displaystyle\\iint_D e^{\\frac{|y|}{|x|+|y|}}\\,d\\sigma$ where $D=\\{(x,y): |x|+|y|\\le 1\\}$.\n\n**Solution:** The integrand depends only on the ratio $|y|/(|x|+|y|)$, and the region is symmetric under $x\\to -x$ and $y\\to -y$. So integrate over the first quadrant and multiply by 4.\n\nIn the first quadrant, $D_1: x+y\\le 1$, $x,y\\ge 0$. Use $u=x+y$, $v=\\frac{y}{x+y}$, so $y=uv$, $x=u(1-v)$, with $0\\le u\\le 1$, $0\\le v\\le 1$. Jacobian: $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)} = \\begin{vmatrix}1-v & -u\\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} = u(1-v)+uv = u$.\n\nSo $\\iint_{D_1} e^{y/(x+y)}dxdy = \\int_0^1\\int_0^1 e^v\\, u\\,dv\\,du = \\frac12\\int_0^1 e^v dv = \\frac12(e-1)$.\n\nTotal: $4\\cdot\\frac12(e-1)=2(e-1)$.",
